course: dictionary
lesson: 9236
title: Soot, the black powder from smoke
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Soot is a word you might meet in science, history or a story. The sentences around it give you clues. «The blast filled the air with dust and soot that darkened the sky» tells you soot is something in the air that makes the sky dark. Soot is not a colour, so you cannot find it by looking up the word black.
explain: The context tells you what soot does. «Soot blackened tree trunks» means soot made the trees black. A good check is to swap in your meaning: the black powder that smoke leaves behind makes sense in every example, so you can trust it.
explain: Some sentences link soot to a time and place. «In England in the 1800s soot blackened tree trunks» tells you soot was common in busy, smoky cities long ago. Words like «flame» and «glowing» nearby show that soot comes from something that burns.
fact: Soot is the black powder that smoke leaves behind when wood, coal, oil or candles burn.
fact: Soot collects inside chimneys, on walls near fires and on cooking pots.
fact: «Soot blackened tree trunks» means soot made the trees black.
fact: In science, soot is made of tiny bits of carbon in smoke, and it can harm your lungs.
fact: Chimney sweeps were children who climbed chimneys to clean out the soot.
exercise: «The blast filled the air with dust and soot that darkened the sky.» What is soot? | *A black powder left behind when things burn | A cloud of rain | A kind of clean, white dust that makes the sky bright | A type of rock thrown up into the sky
exercise: Which of these leaves soot behind? | *A burning candle | A cold tap running with water | Sunlight on a pond | Ice melting in the sun
exercise: «Soot blackened tree trunks, so dark peppered moths hid better.» What did soot do to the tree trunks? | *Made them black | Made them grow taller | Washed them clean | Made them shiny and white
test: «A flame is glowing hot gas; its yellow light comes from tiny specks of hot soot.» Where does the yellow light come from? | The melting wax | The cold air around the flame | The wick of the candle | *Tiny specks of hot soot
test: Why can soot be harmful? | It keeps the air cool | It makes bread rise | It makes your teeth shine | *It can harm your lungs
exam: Mia says soot is a sweet treat from the bakery. What is soot? | A sweet treat from the bakery | *A black powder left behind when wood, coal or oil burns | Pieces of ice | A white powder used to clean windows
